# ── Narrative / process logs — UNTRACKED 2026-08-13 (privacy) ────────────────────
# These narrate real interactions with real people and are where every leak to date
# has happened: an org name in TIMELINE, a person's name x3 in BACKLOG, and two more
# introduced the same day they were being audited. reports/ additionally published
# Alkama's OWN activity data (ambient questions print which apps he used, how often,
# and which hours he was active) into a public repo.
# The public repo keeps the PRODUCT: code + CLAUDE.md. Process logs stay local.
TIMELINE.md
BACKLOG.md
reports/

# ── SECRET/BACKUP GAP CLOSED 2026-08-21 ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Found during commit prep: `.env` was ignored but `.env.backup.<date>` was NOT, so
# `core_logic/.env.backup.2026-08-12` and `.env.backup.2026-08-14` were both fully
# committable to a PUBLIC repo. Between them they carry six of Alkama's own API keys
# (DeepSeek, Gemini, Tavily, XAI), two Telegram bot tokens, and — far worse —
# PARTNER_A / PARTNER_B / PARTNER_C API keys, endpoints, agent ids, an agent private
# key and a pubkey. Those are live THIRD-PARTY credentials covered by signed NDAs.
# Nothing was ever staged or committed; the gap was closed before the first commit in
# eight days. Patterns below are deliberately broad: any env variant, any .bak, any
# memory backup, any NDA or signed legal PDF.
.env*
*.env
core_logic/.env*
*.bak
*.bak.*
*.backup
*.backup.*
core_logic/memory.backup*
*NDA*.pdf
*Nda*.pdf
*_SIGNED_*.pdf

# ── BRIEF ARTIFACTS — kept LOCAL 2026-08-21 (Alkama) ────────────────────────────
# The CLARA brief is an outbound sales/partner deliverable, not product source. It is
# sent per-contact and revised often, and versioned PDFs in a public repo age badly and
# invite people to read a stale pitch. Keeps working copies, extraction junk and the
# rebuild wreckage out of git while leaving every file on disk.
CLARA_Brief_v*.pdf
CLARA_Brief_v*.html
CLARA_Brief_v*.md
CLARA_Brief_v*_TEXT_CHANGES.md
CLARA_Brief_v*_TEXTONLY_BROKEN*.pdf
CLARA_diagram_extracted_*.png
COMMIT_MSG_*.txt

# Delivery artifacts for the fixture: byte-identical copies made for a channel that
# rejects .py. They would drift from the source the moment fixture.py changes, and the
# manifest hash verifies against fixture.py directly, so the repo keeps the source only.
authority_substitution_fixture/frozen_*/

# BRIEF_65 retention archive. Everything moved here came from a gitignored zone and must stay
# gitignored. WITHOUT this rule it depends on luck: reports/, traces/, My_Schedule/ and
# busy-mode-reports/ are unanchored patterns that happen to match at any depth, but benchmarks/
# and logs/ are NOT, so archiving an aged benchmark would have put it in a PUBLIC repo as a
# tracked file. Measured before the first --apply, while only reports/ had anything old enough.
_archive/

# Credential-shaped filenames that escape the `.env*` and `*.bak.*` rules. Probed 2026-09-18:
# `.env.old`, `.env.backup`, `.env.save`, `.env.2026-09-18` and `.env copy` are all already covered,
# but a dot-less dump like `env.txt` was NOT. That is a plausible way to hand someone your keys.
env.txt
env.bak
envs.txt
secrets.txt
*.env.txt
